        "locator": "printed pp. 247–249, section 5.1 and Figures 2–4",
        "role": "supports",
        "sourceId": "arnalds-eydal-gislason-leave-source",
      },
    ],
    "id": "iceland-care-work-outcomes",
    "statementKind": "observation",
    "text": "Surveys of Icelandic first-time parents found that fathers performed a larger share of care after the leave reform.",
  },
  {
    "citations": [
      {
        "locator": "printed p. 250, section 5.2 and Figure 5",
        "role": "supports",
        "sourceId": "arnalds-eydal-gislason-leave-source",
      },
    ],
    "id": "iceland-labor-force-participation-gap",
    "statementKind": "observation",
    "text": "Across the surveyed cohorts, the difference between mothers' and fathers' employment rates three years after their first child's birth became smaller.",
  },
  {
    "citations": [
      {
        "locator": "printed pp. 250–251, section 5.2 and Figure 6",
        "role": "supports",
        "sourceId": "arnalds-eydal-gislason-leave-source",
      },
    ],
    "id": "iceland-working-hours-gap",
    "statementKind": "observation",
    "text": "Across the surveyed cohorts, mothers' average paid working hours increased while fathers' average paid working hours decreased.",
  },
  {
    "citations": [
      {
        "locator": "chapter 18, pp. 370–387, A Policy Success? §§ Programmatic success and Counterclaims to success",
        "role": "supports",
        "sourceId": "gislason-iceland-leave-source",
      },
    ],
    "id": "iceland-causal-transfer-limit",
    "statementKind": "observation",
    "text": "The selected Iceland studies do not isolate the leave design from every concurrent social change.",
  },
  {
    "citations": [
      {
        "locator": "chapter 18, pp. 378–379, ‘An Enduring System’, subsection ‘Coping with a financial crisis’, especially the paragraph reporting the largest decrease among high-income fathers",
        "role": "supports",
        "sourceId": "gislason-iceland-leave-source",
      },
    ],
    "id": "iceland-payment-cuts-uptake",
    "statementKind": "observation",
    "text": "Payment cuts during Iceland's financial crisis were associated with reduced leave use by fathers, with the largest drop among fathers with high incomes.",
  },
]
`;

